WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.190
- No one likes paying higher prices,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.960
which is why for most of 2022

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.030
inflation has been one
of the biggest issues

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.170
on Americans' minds.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.500
- This historic bout of
inflation is primarily the result

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.340
of how the COVID-19 pandemic
upset the global economy,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.800
exacerbated by the war in Ukraine.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.870
The Fed has been raising interest rates

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.630
in an effort to pump the
brakes on rising prices.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.680
And if it works, we should
finally see the economic legacy

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.900
of COVID fade into the past.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.630
- However, there's something
else driving price hikes

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.020
that's far beyond the reach
of the central bank to solve,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.160
climate change.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.361
I'm sure you've heard plenty
about how a warming planet

00:00:37.361 --> 00:00:39.930
will hurt polar bears and coral reefs,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.180
and beach houses on the Florida coast,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.841
but the economic impacts of climate change

00:00:43.841 --> 00:00:46.050
will trickle down to everyone.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.760
Even if you don't live in a flood zone

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.764
your pocketbook will get soaked.

00:00:49.764 --> 00:00:52.931
(light upbeat music)

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.020
- There are three main categories of costs

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.990
that climate change will
affect, and the first is energy.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.259
Here in Texas, air
conditioning is ubiquitous

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:07.620
and it's pretty much a given

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.240
that you'll be running it nonstop

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.980
from May to September.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.140
But as global temperatures rise,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.910
many other regions and cities

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.903
that don't normally rely so heavily on AC

00:01:16.903 --> 00:01:19.170
will start using more and more electricity

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.820
to cool their homes.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.650
This will be offset somewhat

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.795
by less demand for heating fuels like oil

00:01:24.795 --> 00:01:26.940
and natural gas in the wintertime,

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.280
but the net effect will be an increase

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.135
in most Americans' energy bills.

00:01:31.135 --> 00:01:33.630
- In fact, it's already happening.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.090
The average monthly
electric bill in the US

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.870
increased by around 10%
in the last 10 years.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.250
Over the same time period,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.090
average monthly consumption fell by 2.3%,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.130
probably due to the growing popularity

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.620
of energy-saving appliances and lighting.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.243
So if it wasn't for green tech,

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.820
the increase would've been even greater.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.060
- Unfortunately, many
economists expect energy prices

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.090
to rise even higher than
market forces would predict

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.440
for two reasons.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.185
One, increasing energy production

00:02:03.185 --> 00:02:05.820
doesn't mean just turning up a dial.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.090
Much of our energy infrastructure
was built decades ago

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.408
with specific limits on capacity.

00:02:11.408 --> 00:02:13.260
To meet increasing demand,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.128
providers will have to
renovate existing structures,

00:02:16.128 --> 00:02:19.980
build new plants, and lay
new cables and pipelines.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.600
All of this costs money,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.730
which they will pass on to the consumers

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.560
in the form of higher prices.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.107
- The second reason is that
most existing power plants

00:02:28.107 --> 00:02:30.780
use water for cooling.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.773
Higher temperatures means more evaporation

00:02:32.773 --> 00:02:35.760
which means less water and warmer water.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.830
And pumping water in from other regions

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.290
requires, you guessed it, energy,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.330
increasing demand even further.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.261
So these plants will likely
run at a lower efficiency

00:02:45.261 --> 00:02:48.510
than they did in the past,
especially in the very places

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.651
where demand for
electricity will be highest.

00:02:50.651 --> 00:02:53.370
- The upshot is that you
can expect to pay more

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.380
to power your home in the future.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.640
Technology built around sustainable
energy like solar panels

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.991
won't get you completely
off the grid anytime soon,

00:03:01.991 --> 00:03:04.710
but it will start making economic sense

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.413
to more and more households,
especially if a federal subsidy

00:03:08.413 --> 00:03:11.400
can help with those installation costs.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.750
- The next sector of the economy

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.310
that will see significant price hikes

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.830
thanks to climate change is food.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.170
Most of that is due to extreme weather.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.730
Even before the war in Ukraine

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.710
upset that country's grain exports,

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.480
the price of wheat had surged

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.970
to its highest level in eight years,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.197
mainly the result of high
temperatures in the US and Canada.

00:03:30.197 --> 00:03:32.591
In fact, as of the summer of 2022,

00:03:32.591 --> 00:03:37.290
more than 95% of the Western
US is in some level of drought.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.690
In 2021, drought and high temperatures

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.110
caused 93% of wheat
produced in Washington state

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.930
to be in poor or very poor condition.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.840
- To make matters worse,
price hikes caused by drought

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.390
can often be self-exacerbating.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.600
For example, when water
is scarce in California,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.940
it drives up the price of thirsty crops,

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.010
like almonds and pistachios.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.190
Perversely, this makes
those crops more lucrative,

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.530
motivating farmers to grow more of them

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.110
and further depleting the water supply.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.471
Economists call this
unfortunate phenomenon

00:04:09.471 --> 00:04:11.815
the tragedy of the commons.

00:04:11.815 --> 00:04:13.980
- But it's not just droughts.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.481
In 2021, less than a year after
coffee prices had recovered

00:04:17.481 --> 00:04:20.250
from a drought in Brazil, they rose again,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.590
this time because it extreme frost.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.570
Here in Texas, in that same year,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.227
a deep freeze caused at least

00:04:26.227 --> 00:04:29.430
$600 million in agricultural losses,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.700
and other extreme weather events
like floods and hurricanes

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.770
can increase the cost of transporting food

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.600
from the farm to your table.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.000
- Of course, as long as
there's been agriculture,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.752
humans have been dealing
with inclement weather.

00:04:41.752 --> 00:04:44.280
If one food source had a bad year,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.680
consumers could supplement with another.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.350
What makes this period so unprecedented

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.339
is that so many crops are
in crisis simultaneously.

00:04:53.339 --> 00:04:55.980
That makes it much harder
for the average person

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.320
to escape the economic effects.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.760
- The third sector of the economy

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.640
that climate change
will upend is insurance,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.340
particularly in property
and casualty insurance

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.610
which protects homes, cars,
and personal belongings.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.570
This sector took in around $1.6 trillion

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.030
in premium payments in 2020 alone.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.060
- Insurance is supposed to
function as a safety net.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.100
If calamity befalls one person,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.110
the collected payments of everyone else

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.450
can save them from financial ruin.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:28.080
But as floods, hurricanes, and
wildfires become more common

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.940
it's changing the math.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.210
Insured losses from
natural disasters in 2021

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.240
reached $130 billion,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.730
almost twice the yearly average.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:42.240
Insurers are responding
by raising their premiums,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.760
with the average US
homeowner insurance payment

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.530
increasing by 11.4% since 2017.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.110
- But if you live near a
coast or in a floodplain,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.990
you might see a much steeper premium hike.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:56.220
Since 1968,

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.070
the federal government has been
subsidizing flood insurance

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.530
through the National
Flood Insurance Program,

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.990
which allows homeowners
in flood-prone areas

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:05.972
to buy insurance at far lower rates

00:06:05.972 --> 00:06:09.090
than what a traditional
insurance company would charge.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.950
In 2019, FEMA announced

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.930
that they would reprice these policies

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.570
to more accurately reflect the risks.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.970
- This is meant to keep
the program solvent

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.500
and discourage new building

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.840
in areas threatened by climate change,

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.300
but it's also a question of fairness.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.930
For decades, middle and low-income
families who live inland

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.210
have been paying more for their insurance

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:32.250
so that wealthy people can pay less

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:33.930
for their mansions on the coast.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.040
How much less?

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.510
According to the New York Times,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.300
some Florida homeowners may eventually pay

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.162
up to 10 times more for
their flood insurance

00:06:42.162 --> 00:06:44.880
once the price change takes effect.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.260
Yowza!

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.750
- Whether it's energy, food, or insurance,

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:50.790
how much more you'll pay in the future

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:53.100
depends a lot on where you live,

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.310
but virtually no one
will get away scot-free.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.360
The global economy is remarkably
versatile and resilient.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.460
But if the pandemic taught us anything,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.980
it's that when a crisis
hits many different sectors

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.990
and economy simultaneously,

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.240
that's when things can get really bad.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.600
And that's exactly what
climate change is poised to do.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.520
- But it's not entirely bleak.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.770
If governments and
industries around the world

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.618
can respond with policies
that cut carbon emissions

00:07:19.618 --> 00:07:22.427
and develop new, more
efficient technologies

00:07:22.427 --> 00:07:25.320
we can still avoid the
worst-case scenario.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.090
- For decades, some have argued

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.910
that fighting climate
change is too expensive,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.100
but that's like deciding
not to spend the money

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.840
to fix a leak in your roof.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.970
Eventually, when the roof caves in,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.790
the damage will cost
far more than the fix.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.640
- I guess the difference is
that if your house is destroyed

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.800
you can still move to another one.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.950
That's not quite the
case with planet Earth.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.380
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:50.820
- Thanks to our patrons

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.520
for keeping Two Cents financially healthy.

